Regarding the categorical variables, the first sub-journey variable was included to eventually represent a different driver behavior in terms of inattention assuming that the driver, when starting to drive, was likely to be focused on the driving task. As shown in Table 1, 60% of the sub-journeys comprised the first sub-journey. Additionally, two categorical variables were included to represent the age and gender of the driver for each sub-journey. As can be seen in Table 1, most of the sub-journeys were driven by men (88% vs. 8% of women) aged between 18 and 65 years-old (77% vs. 9% were above 65 years-old). Finally, in 4% and 14% of the sub-journeys, there was no information about the driver’s gender and age, respectively.
Regarding the alert events, in 13% of the sub-journeys, at least one alert of drowsiness was registered. In addition, in 52% of the sub-journeys, at least one alert of distraction was recorded. It can be concluded that most of the sub-journeys were driven without any driver drowsiness event, but in contrast in almost half of the sub-journeys a distraction event was detected.
3. Methodology
A binary model was used to assess the relationship between key factors and the probability associated with the occurrence of a drowsiness/distraction event. Considering a binary response and the aforementioned variables, which are gathered in vector, x, the probabilities are represented as follows:
(1)
ProbY=1|x=F(x,β)ProbY=0|x=1-F(x,β)
A set of parameters, β, reflects the impact of changes in x on the probability. A suitable model for F(x,β) is assumed, usually considering a normal or logistic distribution. The question of which distribution to use has been widely analyzed, concluding that the two distributions tend to give similar probabilities except in the tails (Ferreira and Couto, 2012, Greene, 2008). According to Greene (2008), in most cases, the choice between these two distributions seems to make little difference. After analyzing both distributions in the present study, the logistic distribution (logit model) was selected because a small improvement in the goodness-of-fit measures was found. Therefore, a logistic distribution was used, giving rise to the following logit model:
(2)
ProbY=1|x=eβx1+eβx=Λ(βx)
where the function Λ(.) is a commonly used notation for the standard logistic distribution function.
4. Results
Two separate models called the Drowsiness Model and Distraction Model were developed using the binary logit model according to Eqs. (1), (2). The categories called 0 of the categorical variables described in Table 1 were selected as the reference category. After running the models, the results showed that the variable journey time and breaking duration time were not statistically significant in both models. Therefore, these variables were removed from the models. The variables that proved to be statistically significant, at least in one of the models, were kept in the models’ results to allow a better comparison of the results of the two models, leading to the results shown in Tables 2 and 3.

