5. Discussion
This study explores ‘opportunistic’ data, which were obtained from a DMS available to any driver owning a smartphone, in order to analyze factors that influence the probability of a drowsiness and distraction event occurring. The studied factors are related to the journey and driver’s characteristics. Despite the fact that various studies have been developed to study driver behavior focusing on drowsiness and distraction, to the best of our knowledge, there are no similar studies to the present one. Additionally, most of the past studies used a driver monitoring system that recorded the driver state but did not alert the driver, and therefore having no impact on the behavior. As mentioned by Fitzharris, Liu, Stephens, and Lenné (2017), when drowsiness feedback was provided to the driver, the number of events decreased significantly. In fact, when feedback is given to the driver (i.e., sounding an alert in this case), the driver can decide to stop and have a rest or a coffee, for example, and, as such, alters the journey concerning the situation in which there is no feedback. Therefore, no direct comparison of findings from other studies can be provided. Nevertheless, the present findings can be related to conclusions from other studies.
In this study, two models were considered to analyze distraction and drowsiness separately. Indeed, the results show some effect differences of the variable on the probabilities of drowsiness and distraction occurring. This is particularly true in the case of variables describing the drivers’ age and gender. The probability of a drowsiness event occurring is highest for older drivers and in contrast, the driver’s age did not show any effect on the probability of a distraction event occurring. On the other hand, the drivers who did not provide gender information (category 2) are related to the probabilities of drowsiness and distraction, however with opposite effects. Apparently, these drivers represent a risk group in terms of drowsiness and, in contrast, they are associated to a lower risk of distraction when compared to male drivers. Unfortunately, no practical guidance can be provided given the content of the information.
Despite the fact that no comparison can be made with other studies and even among past studies, which also investigated the impact of age, because there are several differences regarding the experimental conditions and age groups that may explain the different results, we have summarized some interesting past findings. For instance, Filtness, Reyner, and Horne (2012) mentioned that following a normal sleep, no differences between age groups were found during simulated driving experiments. Even after sleep restriction, younger drivers showed significantly more sleepiness-related measures, leading to the conclusion that younger drivers have greater vulnerability to sleep loss when driving for a long time in the afternoon. In addition, a crash analysis conducted by Tefft (2010) showed that younger drivers aged 16–24 were nearly twice as likely to be involved in a drowsy driving crash as drivers aged 40–59. In the case of driver distraction, different kinds of conclusions were found eventually depending on the type of analyzed distraction. For instance, Strayer and Drews (2004) found that the driving performance of both younger and older adults was influenced by cell phone conversations with equivalent effects. Additionally, Guo et al. (2017) concluded that visual–manual distractions impact drivers of all ages, whereas cognitive distraction may have a larger impact on young drivers. Along the same lines, Pope, Bell, and Stavrinos (2017) reported that distracted driving is a ubiquitous phenomenon evident in drivers of all ages. The gender role was also analyzed in various studies. In the case of drowsiness, Tefft (2010) found that two out of three drivers involved in a drowsy driving crash were men. Curiously, Obst, Armstrong, Smith, and Banks (2011) concluded, based on a survey, that risk taking behavior with regards to driving while sleepy occurred in both male and female drivers and across all age groups. However, young to middle aged drivers and male drivers reported the highest frequency of driving while sleepy and reported the lowest perceived personal risk regarding driving while sleepy.
In the case of distraction, few studies were found that focused on the gender issue of the driver, however in the ESRA project (Trigoso, Areal, & Pires, 2016) where an online questionnaire was conducted at European level, it was found that women and older people are more aware of the risks of using a mobile phone while driving and do it less. Green et al. (2007) conducted a naturalistic driving study (96 drivers, 136,792 miles) under the SAVE-IT project to determine how distracted and normal driving differ. The study results revealed that men were more distracted (56% of the frames) than women (44%).
Considering the variables describing the journey, similar trends between drowsiness and distraction can be set out based on both models’ results. It seems clear that increasing the continuous driving time, the probabilities of either a distraction or drowsiness occurring also increase. These findings are supported by several studies particularly in the case of drowsiness research in which the time length of the driving task is always a research issue, especially in the case of professional drivers (Anund et al., 2016, Di Milia and Kecklund, 2013, Mahajan et al., 2019, Pylkkönen et al., 2015). Indeed, a previous study focusing on professional drivers, in which similar data obtained from a DMS were used (Ferreira et al., 2019), clearly shows this effect. Nevertheless, other studies on non-professional drivers were conducted such as a study by Van Der Hulst, Meijman, and Rothengatter (2001) in which a driving simulator was used showing that fatigue, which was associated with deterioration of perceptual-motor performance, increased over time. Moreover, Wang, Bie, and Li (2016) found that when driving time is increased, the average heart rate of drivers increases as well, implying that the active fatigue level of the driver becomes worse. In the case of driver distraction, no specific studies focusing on this variable were found in a simple search. Nevertheless, Iseland, Johansson, Skoog, and Dåderman (2018) reported that long-haul truck drivers feel bored and use secondary tasks as a coping strategy to alleviate boredom/drowsiness, and for social interaction. These secondary tasks are described as work environment related “necessities” (e.g., getting food and/or beverages from the refrigerator/bag, eating, drinking, removing a jacket, rubbing their face, and adjusting the seat), interacting with a mobile phone/in-truck technology, and doing administrative tasks. Overall, driving time can be assumed as an exposure variable to the risk of drowsiness and distraction occurrence. On the other hand, if the driver stops, and regardless of why he/she stopped, the probabilities of drowsiness and distraction occurrence decrease. Again, regarding driver distraction, no particular study was found focusing on the effect of a journey break on this driver state, however in the case of drowsiness, studies have been published particularly focusing on professional drivers, showing the same kind of results (Bunn et al., 2017, Ferreira et al., 2019, Mahajan et al., 2019).
Overall, the findings of the present study related to the sub-journey time and number of breaks variables are in agreement with the idea of the load of the driving task. Interestingly, the journey time (i.e., the total driving time) and the break duration time did not show any effect on both driver states, leading to the conclusion that the continuous driving time and stopping during the journey are the main factors contributing to the phenomenon. It is interesting to note that the first sub-journey affects the driver distraction differently, but with no impact on drowsiness. In the former, the probability of a distraction event occurring during the first sub-journey is lower than in the other following sub-journeys. It seems that, in the case of distraction, the driver is more concentrated on the driving task during the first sub-journey, and after that the driver starts to be more prone to other tasks, maybe because he/she has become bored. In contrast, drowsiness seems to be a driver behavior mostly affected by the continuous driving time, either represented by the sub-journey time and number of breaks, regardless of the sub-journey sequence.
In order to better understand the magnitude of the effects of the driving time and number of breaks variables, the probabilities were computed for different scenarios assuming the mean values of each variable (see Table 1) and the 0 category for the categorical variables for the reference scenario. The partial effect is computed by comparing the probabilities of the analyzed scenario (Sa) with the reference scenario (Sr), i.e. (Sa −  r)/Sr. Two scenarios, besides the reference scenario, are analyzed and described in Table 4: one assuming an increase of 20% of the sub-journey time and the other considering one stop instead of non-stop.

